Colour Palette:
For this assignment, I decided not to use one main colour theme, but instead use colour in a more functional and visually appealing way. I 
started with a very light base using whites, greys, and soft tones because I wanted it to look clean and easy to read. From there, I used 
specific colours to represent different task statuses. The task bars are colour-coded based on status: red for overdue since it feels the most 
urgent, yellow for due soon as more of a warning, blue for on track because it feels calmer, and green for completed to show that the task is 
done. For the boards, I wanted different versions of the colours and gave users the option to choose their own board colours so they can 
customize it to their preference or match a certain theme if they want. I also included a priority system where tasks can be marked as high, 
medium, or low. Since the task bars are already coloured based on status, I added a small coloured dot next to the task name to represent 
priority instead. This uses red for high, yellow for medium, and blue for low, using slightly darker shades so they still stand out. I was 
inspired by Notion for this idea, since they use coloured dots to represent different categories like assignments or exams, and I adapted that 
concept for priority instead. Lastly, I wanted to match the colours of the counters at the top with the same colours used for task statuses and 
boards so everything stays consistent across the entire app.

Overall Style & Theme:
For the overall style of my app, I was really inspired by modern tools and applications. Some of my favourites that I took inspiration from are 
Notion, Google Reminders and Chalk, which is an organizers for teachers that my sister introduced me too. A lot of my friends also mentioned 
that they prefer Notions style, so that was what I going for. I also took inspiration from Kanban-style layouts like Trello, where everything 
is organized into boards (Per the assignments requests). I wanted the design to feel clean, simple, and easy to use rather than overly styled 
or complicated to look at, so I used rounded cards, and expandable task sections, similar to Trellos, so the app doesn’t feel cluttered.

Outside of the required functions for the assignment, I wanted to add more functionalities that can make this app stand out and feel more like 
kanban task manager. For example, I added an Upcoming Tasks section that shows tasks based on due date and priority. It automatically sorts 
tasks by the closest due date first, and if tasks have the same date, it orders them by priority. It also excludes completed tasks or tasks 
without dates so users can focus on what still needs to be done. I liked this idea as it serves kind of like a reminder for the users of whats 
to be done first. I also created a Completed board that is automatically added. Instead of just deleting a task when its done, I thought making 
a completed board and marking a task visually as done would fit better. I added a “Done” button that actually moves the task into the Completed 
board but you can also just drag the task there as well. This helps keep the main boards clean and makes it easier to separate finished tasks 
from active tasks.

To make the app easier to use, I added a search bar and a filter, so users can quickly find tasks or filter them by status. I also used 
expandable task cards. I orignally had the tasks shoiwng all the infomation underneath and thought it looked too big and too much, and I noticed 
on Trello they had expandable options to view more actions and thought it was a good idea. So now if users click on a task they can see more 
details like description, due date, priority, and notes, instead of showing everything at once. It also were the edit, delete, and done button 
can be found for the tasks. 

